A state is unsafe if:
- {C, F} ⊆ far side and {D, L} ⊆ near side (i.e., far side has exactly C and F)
  OR equivalently: near side has exactly {D, L} and far side has exactly {C, F}
- {D, L} ⊆ far side and {C, F} ⊆ near side (i.e., far side has exactly D and L)
  OR equivalently: near side has exactly {C, F} and far side has exactly {D, L}

Let me label states:
S0 = {}
S1 = {C}
S2 = {F}
S3 = {D}
S4 = {L}
S5 = {C,D}
S6 = {C,L}
S7 = {F,D}
S8 = {F,L}
S9 = {C,F,D}
S10 = {C,F,L}
S11 = {C,D,L}
S12 = {F,D,L}
S13 = {C,F,D,L} (goal, E = 0)

Actually, the symmetry is: swap C↔F and D↔L. Under this swap:
  - Cat eats fish constraint ↔ Dog eats lunch constraint (both are "forbidden pairs")
  - {C,D} → {F,L}
  - {C,L} → {F,D}
  - {F,D} → {C,L}
  - {F,L} → {C,D}

And under C↔F swap:
- {C} ↔ {F}
- {D} ↔ {D} (no change)
- {L} ↔ {L} (no change)
- {C,D} ↔ {F,D}
- {C,L} ↔ {F,L}
- {F,D} ↔ {C,D}
- {F,L} ↔ {C,L}
- {C,F,D} ↔ {C,F,D} (no change, C and F both present)
- {C,F,L} ↔ {C,F,L} (no change)
- {C,D,L} ↔ {F,D,L}
- {F,D,L} ↔ {C,D,L}

Wait, let me double-check the class for single items. From {C}:
- Valid moves: to {}, {C,D}, {C,L}
- Probabilities: 1/3 each

From {D}:
- Valid moves: to {C,D}, {F,D}, {}
- Probabilities: 1/3 each
